  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "The two acute angles of a right triangle differ by \\(20^\\circ\\). How many degrees is the larger acute angle?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "55",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Let the two acute angles be \\(x\\) and \\(x+20\\). Then \\(2x+20=90\\), so \\(x=35\\), and the larger acute angle is \\(55^\\circ\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Use the average and half the difference\n\nThe two acute angles sum to \\(90^\\circ\\), so their average is \\(90^\\circ\\div2=45^\\circ\\). The larger and smaller measures lie equally far above and below their average. Their \\(20^\\circ\\) difference is twice this distance, so the larger acute angle is \\(45^\\circ+20^\\circ\\div2=55^\\circ\\).",
